{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016pT65Gkaj8L4TB4nruMwUF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texperiments:\n2\t  # === P0: Headline Results (must run) ===\n3\t\n4\t  - id: plan_001\n5\t    name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n6\t    claim: 1\n7\t    description: >\n8\t      Apply DIF analysis to METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items).\n9\t      Data characteristics: only 37% models classifiable by family (1,929); effective temporal\n10\t      span ~1.5 years (2023: N=1,253, 2024-H1: N=656); 15.4% items have negative discrimination.\n11\t      Design:\n12\t      (a) Primary cohort: temporal split (2023 vs 2024-H1, both N≥80 ✓)\n13\t      (b) Alternative cohort: accuracy-quintile split (ability-based grouping, uses all models)\n14\t      (c) Item filtering: run with and without negative-discrimination items (sensitivity analysis)\n15\t      Run ETS C DIF detection on all items under both cohort definitions.\n16\t      Proxy validation against Li et al. (EMNLP 2024) web-overlap contamination labels (9,229 items,\n17\t      73.8% coverage). IMPORTANT conceptual distinction: Li et al. labels are model-agnostic\n18\t      (item exists on web), while DIF detects cohort-differential contamination (item behaves\n19\t      differently across temporal cohorts). Low concordance is EXPECTED — DIF only catches items\n20\t      where contamination is differential across cohorts, not uniform. Primary validation metric:\n21\t      FPR analysis (DIF flags on Li-et-al-clean items) rather than TPR (which conflates uniform\n22\t      vs differential contamination). Unlabeled items (26.2%) reported separately as \"unknown\".\n23\t      plan_011 (semi-synthetic injection) provides controlled ground truth to complement this proxy.\n24\t      (d) Within-family confound control: run DIF within model families where both versions\n25\t          have N≥80. Confirmed feasible: Llama-2 (N=161) vs Llama-3 (N=308) ✓. Qwen-1.5 (N=67)\n26\t          below threshold — use as supplementary only. Identify additional qualifying pairs from\n27\t          METABENCH metadata. If DIF flags\n28\t          items that are NOT differentially contaminated within-family, the flag reflects capability\n29\t          gain, not contamination. Report: DIF flag overlap between cross-cohort and within-family\n30\t          analyses to disentangle contamination from capability improvement.\n31\t      (e) Domain-specificity analysis: correlate DIF flags with MMLU subject categories to check\n32\t          whether flags cluster in domains with known capability jumps vs known contamination.\n33\t      Report: DIF flags × cohort definition × item filtering — 4-way sensitivity matrix +\n34\t      within-family confound control + domain-specificity analysis.\n35\t    priority: P0\n36\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n37\t    deps: []\n38\t    registry_id: \"\"\n39\t\n40\t  - id: plan_002\n41\t    name: rl_robustness_validation\n42\t    claim: 1\n43\t    description: >\n44\t      Empirically validate DIF's RL-robustness claim. CRITICAL DISTINCTION:\n45\t      - \"Alignment RL\" (DPO/ORPO/KTO) = offline preference optimization, does NOT produce\n46\t        the NLL gap contraction that Wang et al. demonstrate. METABENCH has 477 such models.\n47\t      - \"Reasoning RL\" (GRPO/PPO online training) = the mechanism that conceals contamination\n48\t        signals. This is what Claim 1 targets. METABENCH has almost none of these.\n49\t      Design (Reviewer-validated):\n50\t      (a) SFT-only cohort from METABENCH as baseline\n51\t      (b) Alignment-RL cohort (DPO/ORPO, N=477) from METABENCH as mechanism control —\n52\t          DPO doesn't use PPO clipping, so no NLL gap contraction; if DIF works on both\n53\t          (a) and (b), robustness covers the full RL spectrum\n54\t      (c) Reasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1-7B/32B, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) — run MMLU inference\n55\t          on 10-15 open-weight GRPO/PPO models. These are ADDED to the focal cohort\n56\t          (not a standalone group): DIF is group-level, so we verify that adding RL models\n57\t          to a cohort doesn't degrade detection. Separately, run traditional detectors\n58\t          (Min-K%, ConStat) on these 10-15 models individually to show per-model degradation.\n59\t      Two-level validation:\n60\t      - Group level: DIF flags same items regardless of cohort composition (a/b/c) → robust\n61\t      - Model level: traditional detectors degrade on (c) individuals → fragile\n62\t      This design works even with only 10-15 reasoning-RL models because DIF doesn't need\n63\t      N≥80 RL models — it needs N≥80 in the cohort total, and RL models are mixed in.\n64\t      Statistical rigor requirements:\n65\t      - Bootstrap 95% CIs on all AUROC comparisons (DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K%)\n66\t      - DeLong test or permutation test for pairwise AUROC differences\n67\t      - TOST equivalence testing for DIF retention ratio vs ConStat (not just null-result reasoning)\n68\t      (d) Partial exploitation power analysis: formally characterize DIF detection power as\n69\t          function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]. At p<1, contaminated model answers\n70\t          memorized items correctly with probability p, shrinking DIF effect size by ~p.\n71\t          Show detection boundary and ensemble coverage handoff point.\n72\t    priority: P0\n73\t    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n74\t    deps: [plan_001]\n75\t    registry_id: \"\"\n76\t\n77\t  - id: plan_003a\n78\t    name: constat_perturbation_eval\n79\t    claim: 2\n80\t    description: >\n81\t      Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU:\n82\t      (a) Generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 200+ MMLU items using LLM paraphrasing\n83\t      (b) Compare model performance on original vs reformulated items\n84\t      (c) Apply FDR-controlled detection (ConStat protocol)\n85\t      (d) Characterize detection power by effect size — expected strength on non-RL models, weakness on RL\n86\t      Even if DIF or DVD fails, ConStat standalone results have paper value as baseline comparison.\n87\t    priority: P0\n88\t    gpu_budget_hours: 80\n89\t    deps: [plan_001]\n90\t    registry_id: \"\"\n91\t\n92\t  - id: plan_003b\n93\t    name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n94\t    claim: 2\n95\t    description: >\n96\t      Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU:\n97\t      (a) Select 10-15 open-weight models with inference access\n98\t      (b) Generate 50 responses per item via temperature sampling (T=0.7-1.0)\n99\t      (c) Compute generation variance scores; detect bimodal distributions (contamination signal)\n100\t      (d) Characterize detection power for variant contamination vs verbatim contamination\n101\t      Standalone results provide variant-contamination detection baseline independent of DIF.\n102\t    priority: P0\n103\t    gpu_budget_hours: 70\n104\t    deps: [plan_001]\n105\t    registry_id: \"\"\n106\t\n107\t  - id: plan_003c\n108\t    name: ensemble_fusion\n109\t    claim: 2\n110\t    description: >\n111\t      Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble:\n112\t      (a) Calibrate per-signal thresholds on subset of items with known contamination status\n113\t      (b) Evaluate 2-of-3 majority voting and weighted fusion strategies\n114\t      (c) Show ensemble coverage: DIF covers δ≥1.4, ConStat covers non-RL δ≥0.6, DVD covers variant\n115\t      (d) Precision analysis: ensemble FPR<0.10 across all configurations\n116\t      (e) Cross-validate fusion weights (5-fold); report uniform-weight baseline alongside optimized\n117\t      Depends on 003a and 003b completing; if one signal fails, degrade to 2-signal ensemble.\n118\t    priority: P0\n119\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n120\t    deps: [plan_001, plan_003a, plan_003b]\n121\t    registry_id: \"\"\n122\t\n123\t  # === P1: Strengthening Evidence ===\n124\t\n125\t  - id: plan_004\n126\t    name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n127\t    claim: 1\n128\t    description: >\n129\t      Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K using GSM1K as ground truth for contamination.\n130\t      Collect per-item binary responses from 50+ models on GSM8K (via existing leaderboard data\n131\t      or inference). Run DIF across temporal cohorts. Correlate DIF flags with\n132\t      per-model GSM8K-GSM1K accuracy gap. Validates cross-benchmark generalizability\n133\t      and provides an independent ground truth beyond Li et al. proxy labels.\n134\t    priority: P1\n135\t    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n136\t    deps: [plan_001]\n137\t    registry_id: \"\"\n138\t\n139\t  - id: plan_005\n140\t    name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n141\t    claim: 3\n142\t    description: >\n143\t      Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU:\n144\t      NOTE: METABENCH preproc.rds contains CTT statistics (p-value, rpbis), NOT IRT parameters.\n145\t      True IRT params are in mirt model objects (crossvalidate.R). Must either extract from\n146\t      METABENCH mirt RDS files or refit from the 5219×12508 response matrix.\n147\t      (a) Fit bifactor model to METABENCH MMLU data (general factor + subject-specific factors)\n148\t      (b) Select anchor items (high-stability, no DIF across cohorts)\n149\t      (c) Calibrate replacement items via fixed-parameter calibration (Kim & Kolen 2019)\n150\t      (d) Demonstrate equated score equivalence: model rankings on original vs replaced benchmark\n151\t      should correlate at Spearman rho ≥ 0.95 after equating.\n152\t    priority: P1\n153\t    gpu_budget_hours: 50\n154\t    deps: [plan_001]\n155\t    registry_id: \"\"\n156\t\n157\t  - id: plan_006\n158\t    name: item_generation_calibration\n159\t    claim: 3\n160\t    description: >\n161\t      Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions:\n162\t      (a) Use LLM generation with construct constraints (same subject, same cognitive level)\n163\t      (b) Calibrate via student simulation (arXiv 2601.09953, r=0.75-0.82 for difficulty estimation)\n164\t      (c) Compare difficulty correlation: prompt-based targeting (rho=0.10-0.45 per arXiv 2602.06631)\n165\t      vs empirical calibration via student simulation\n166\t      (d) Validate on holdout model panel\n167\t    priority: P1\n168\t    gpu_budget_hours: 200\n169\t    deps: [plan_005]\n170\t    registry_id: \"\"\n171\t\n172\t  # === P2: Bonus / Extension ===\n173\t\n174\t  - id: plan_007\n175\t    name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n176\t    claim: 1\n177\t    description: >\n178\t      Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006).\n179\t      Simulate 4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power improvement\n180\t      from accumulating evidence: individual administration power ~0.40\n181\t      → cumulative power ~0.70 after 3 administrations for medium effects (δ=1.0).\n182\t    priority: P2\n183\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n184\t    deps: [plan_001]\n185\t    registry_id: \"\"\n186\t\n187\t  - id: plan_008\n188\t    name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n189\t    claim: 3\n190\t    description: >\n191\t      Formal analysis of benchmark validity under item replacement:\n192\t      (a) Define validity metrics (ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage)\n193\t      (b) Simulate progressive replacement (5%, 10%, 20%, 50% items replaced)\n194\t      (c) Establish theoretical bounds on validity degradation as function of replacement rate\n195\t      (d) Empirical validation using METABENCH data\n196\t    priority: P2\n197\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n198\t    deps: [plan_005, plan_006]\n199\t    registry_id: \"\"\n200\t\n201\t  - id: plan_009\n202\t    name: item_focused_trees\n203\t    claim: 1\n204\t    description: >\n205\t      Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to\n206\t      categorical cohort DIF. Treat model release date as continuous covariate.\n207\t      Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical DIF.\n208\t      Advantages: avoids arbitrary cohort splits, uses full model sample.\n209\t    priority: P2\n210\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n211\t    deps: [plan_001]\n212\t    registry_id: \"\"\n213\t\n214\t  - id: plan_010\n215\t    name: architecture_stratified_dif\n216\t    claim: 1\n217\t    description: >\n218\t      Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations to test whether\n219\t      IRT model fit improves and spurious DIF reduces when architecture heterogeneity\n220\t      is removed. Dream worst-case identified this as the critical missing experiment.\n221\t      Design:\n222\t      (a) Subset METABENCH to decoder-only models only (N≈3,000-4,000)\n223\t      (b) Report IRT fit diagnostics (RMSEA, CFI, TLI, SRMR) for this homogeneous subgroup\n224\t      (c) Run DIF detection on decoder-only temporal cohorts; compare FPR and TPR\n225\t          vs full-population DIF from plan_001\n226\t      (d) If fit improves substantially, conclusion is \"DIF requires architecture-homogeneous\n227\t          populations\" — a fixable constraint, not a fundamental limitation\n228\t      Zero GPU cost (uses existing METABENCH data). Proactive defense against worst-case\n229\t      reviewer concern about unidimensional IRT misfit.\n230\t    priority: P1\n231\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n232\t    deps: [plan_001]\n233\t    registry_id: \"\"\n234\t\n235\t  - id: plan_011\n236\t    name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n237\t    claim: 1\n238\t    description: >\n239\t      Inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix and measure\n240\t      DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Addresses ground truth\n241\t      circularity concern (Li et al. proxy labels measure existence, not differential contamination).\n242\t      Design:\n243\t      (a) Select 500 items with known-clean status (no DIF flags, stable across cohorts)\n244\t      (b) For a random subset of 100-200 items, inject synthetic contamination:\n245\t          flip responses of focal-group models from incorrect→correct with probability p\n246\t          (simulating contamination at varying exploitation rates)\n247\t      (c) Run DIF detection; measure TPR/FPR against known-injected ground truth\n248\t      (d) Vary injection parameters: contamination prevalence (5%-30%), exploitation rate\n249\t          (0.4-1.0), focal group size (N=40-200)\n250\t      Provides controlled ground truth that proxy labels (Li et al.) and GSM1K cannot.\n251\t    priority: P1\n252\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n253\t    deps: [plan_001]\n254\t    registry_id: \"\"\n255\t\n256\t# GPU Budget Summary:\n257\t# P0: 250 hours (plan_001: 0h + plan_002: 100h + plan_003a: 80h + plan_003b: 70h + plan_003c: 0h)\n258\t# P1: 350 hours (plan_004: 100h + plan_005: 50h + plan_006: 200h + plan_010: 0h + plan_011: 0h)\n259\t# P2: 0 hours (all simulation/analysis)\n260\t# Total: 600 hours (within 900-1200h project budget, leaving margin for reruns)\n261\t# Note: plan_010 (arch-stratified DIF) and plan_011 (semi-synthetic validation) added post-Dream,\n262\t# both 0 GPU-h (CPU-only analysis on existing METABENCH data)\n263\t# Claim 2 resilience: if one signal fails, 2-signal ensemble still publishable\n264\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff7feff5-12f0-4f3d-8a05-edb61f233b1a","uuid":"de35d9be-58a3-49c2-874a-1bf268826b13","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:23:56.166Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/experiment_plan.yaml","content":"experiments:\n  # === P0: Headline Results (must run) ===\n\n  - id: plan_001\n    name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Apply DIF analysis to METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items).\n      Data characteristics: only 37% models classifiable by family (1,929); effective temporal\n      span ~1.5 years (2023: N=1,253, 2024-H1: N=656); 15.4% items have negative discrimination.\n      Design:\n      (a) Primary cohort: temporal split (2023 vs 2024-H1, both N≥80 ✓)\n      (b) Alternative cohort: accuracy-quintile split (ability-based grouping, uses all models)\n      (c) Item filtering: run with and without negative-discrimination items (sensitivity analysis)\n      Run ETS C DIF detection on all items under both cohort definitions.\n      Proxy validation against Li et al. (EMNLP 2024) web-overlap contamination labels (9,229 items,\n      73.8% coverage). IMPORTANT conceptual distinction: Li et al. labels are model-agnostic\n      (item exists on web), while DIF detects cohort-differential contamination (item behaves\n      differently across temporal cohorts). Low concordance is EXPECTED — DIF only catches items\n      where contamination is differential across cohorts, not uniform. Primary validation metric:\n      FPR analysis (DIF flags on Li-et-al-clean items) rather than TPR (which conflates uniform\n      vs differential contamination). Unlabeled items (26.2%) reported separately as \"unknown\".\n      plan_011 (semi-synthetic injection) provides controlled ground truth to complement this proxy.\n      (d) Within-family confound control: run DIF within model families where both versions\n          have N≥80. Confirmed feasible: Llama-2 (N=161) vs Llama-3 (N=308) ✓. Qwen-1.5 (N=67)\n          below threshold — use as supplementary only. Identify additional qualifying pairs from\n          METABENCH metadata. If DIF flags\n          items that are NOT differentially contaminated within-family, the flag reflects capability\n          gain, not contamination. Report: DIF flag overlap between cross-cohort and within-family\n          analyses to disentangle contamination from capability improvement.\n      (e) Domain-specificity analysis: correlate DIF flags with MMLU subject categories to check\n          whether flags cluster in domains with known capability jumps vs known contamination.\n      Report: DIF flags × cohort definition × item filtering — 4-way sensitivity matrix +\n      within-family confound control + domain-specificity analysis.\n    priority: P0\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: []\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_002\n    name: rl_robustness_validation\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Empirically validate DIF's RL-robustness claim. CRITICAL DISTINCTION:\n      - \"Alignment RL\" (DPO/ORPO/KTO) = offline preference optimization, does NOT produce\n        the NLL gap contraction that Wang et al. demonstrate. METABENCH has 477 such models.\n      - \"Reasoning RL\" (GRPO/PPO online training) = the mechanism that conceals contamination\n        signals. This is what Claim 1 targets. METABENCH has almost none of these.\n      Design (Reviewer-validated):\n      (a) SFT-only cohort from METABENCH as baseline\n      (b) Alignment-RL cohort (DPO/ORPO, N=477) from METABENCH as mechanism control —\n          DPO doesn't use PPO clipping, so no NLL gap contraction; if DIF works on both\n          (a) and (b), robustness covers the full RL spectrum\n      (c) Reasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1-7B/32B, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) — run MMLU inference\n          on 10-15 open-weight GRPO/PPO models. These are ADDED to the focal cohort\n          (not a standalone group): DIF is group-level, so we verify that adding RL models\n          to a cohort doesn't degrade detection. Separately, run traditional detectors\n          (Min-K%, ConStat) on these 10-15 models individually to show per-model degradation.\n      Two-level validation:\n      - Group level: DIF flags same items regardless of cohort composition (a/b/c) → robust\n      - Model level: traditional detectors degrade on (c) individuals → fragile\n      This design works even with only 10-15 reasoning-RL models because DIF doesn't need\n      N≥80 RL models — it needs N≥80 in the cohort total, and RL models are mixed in.\n      Statistical rigor requirements:\n      - Bootstrap 95% CIs on all AUROC comparisons (DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K%)\n      - DeLong test or permutation test for pairwise AUROC differences\n      - TOST equivalence testing for DIF retention ratio vs ConStat (not just null-result reasoning)\n      (d) Partial exploitation power analysis: formally characterize DIF detection power as\n          function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]. At p<1, contaminated model answers\n          memorized items correctly with probability p, shrinking DIF effect size by ~p.\n          Show detection boundary and ensemble coverage handoff point.\n    priority: P0\n    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_003a\n    name: constat_perturbation_eval\n    claim: 2\n    description: >\n      Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU:\n      (a) Generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 200+ MMLU items using LLM paraphrasing\n      (b) Compare model performance on original vs reformulated items\n      (c) Apply FDR-controlled detection (ConStat protocol)\n      (d) Characterize detection power by effect size — expected strength on non-RL models, weakness on RL\n      Even if DIF or DVD fails, ConStat standalone results have paper value as baseline comparison.\n    priority: P0\n    gpu_budget_hours: 80\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_003b\n    name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n    claim: 2\n    description: >\n      Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU:\n      (a) Select 10-15 open-weight models with inference access\n      (b) Generate 50 responses per item via temperature sampling (T=0.7-1.0)\n      (c) Compute generation variance scores; detect bimodal distributions (contamination signal)\n      (d) Characterize detection power for variant contamination vs verbatim contamination\n      Standalone results provide variant-contamination detection baseline independent of DIF.\n    priority: P0\n    gpu_budget_hours: 70\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_003c\n    name: ensemble_fusion\n    claim: 2\n    description: >\n      Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble:\n      (a) Calibrate per-signal thresholds on subset of items with known contamination status\n      (b) Evaluate 2-of-3 majority voting and weighted fusion strategies\n      (c) Show ensemble coverage: DIF covers δ≥1.4, ConStat covers non-RL δ≥0.6, DVD covers variant\n      (d) Precision analysis: ensemble FPR<0.10 across all configurations\n      (e) Cross-validate fusion weights (5-fold); report uniform-weight baseline alongside optimized\n      Depends on 003a and 003b completing; if one signal fails, degrade to 2-signal ensemble.\n    priority: P0\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_001, plan_003a, plan_003b]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  # === P1: Strengthening Evidence ===\n\n  - id: plan_004\n    name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K using GSM1K as ground truth for contamination.\n      Collect per-item binary responses from 50+ models on GSM8K (via existing leaderboard data\n      or inference). Run DIF across temporal cohorts. Correlate DIF flags with\n      per-model GSM8K-GSM1K accuracy gap. Validates cross-benchmark generalizability\n      and provides an independent ground truth beyond Li et al. proxy labels.\n    priority: P1\n    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_005\n    name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n    claim: 3\n    description: >\n      Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU:\n      NOTE: METABENCH preproc.rds contains CTT statistics (p-value, rpbis), NOT IRT parameters.\n      True IRT params are in mirt model objects (crossvalidate.R). Must either extract from\n      METABENCH mirt RDS files or refit from the 5219×12508 response matrix.\n      (a) Fit bifactor model to METABENCH MMLU data (general factor + subject-specific factors)\n      (b) Select anchor items (high-stability, no DIF across cohorts)\n      (c) Calibrate replacement items via fixed-parameter calibration (Kim & Kolen 2019)\n      (d) Demonstrate equated score equivalence: model rankings on original vs replaced benchmark\n      should correlate at Spearman rho ≥ 0.95 after equating.\n    priority: P1\n    gpu_budget_hours: 50\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_006\n    name: item_generation_calibration\n    claim: 3\n    description: >\n      Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions:\n      (a) Use LLM generation with construct constraints (same subject, same cognitive level)\n      (b) Calibrate via student simulation (arXiv 2601.09953, r=0.75-0.82 for difficulty estimation)\n      (c) Compare difficulty correlation: prompt-based targeting (rho=0.10-0.45 per arXiv 2602.06631)\n      vs empirical calibration via student simulation\n      (d) Validate on holdout model panel\n    priority: P1\n    gpu_budget_hours: 200\n    deps: [plan_005]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  # === P2: Bonus / Extension ===\n\n  - id: plan_007\n    name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006).\n      Simulate 4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power improvement\n      from accumulating evidence: individual administration power ~0.40\n      → cumulative power ~0.70 after 3 administrations for medium effects (δ=1.0).\n    priority: P2\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_008\n    name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n    claim: 3\n    description: >\n      Formal analysis of benchmark validity under item replacement:\n      (a) Define validity metrics (ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage)\n      (b) Simulate progressive replacement (5%, 10%, 20%, 50% items replaced)\n      (c) Establish theoretical bounds on validity degradation as function of replacement rate\n      (d) Empirical validation using METABENCH data\n    priority: P2\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_005, plan_006]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_009\n    name: item_focused_trees\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to\n      categorical cohort DIF. Treat model release date as continuous covariate.\n      Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical DIF.\n      Advantages: avoids arbitrary cohort splits, uses full model sample.\n    priority: P2\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_010\n    name: architecture_stratified_dif\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations to test whether\n      IRT model fit improves and spurious DIF reduces when architecture heterogeneity\n      is removed. Dream worst-case identified this as the critical missing experiment.\n      Design:\n      (a) Subset METABENCH to decoder-only models only (N≈3,000-4,000)\n      (b) Report IRT fit diagnostics (RMSEA, CFI, TLI, SRMR) for this homogeneous subgroup\n      (c) Run DIF detection on decoder-only temporal cohorts; compare FPR and TPR\n          vs full-population DIF from plan_001\n      (d) If fit improves substantially, conclusion is \"DIF requires architecture-homogeneous\n          populations\" — a fixable constraint, not a fundamental limitation\n      Zero GPU cost (uses existing METABENCH data). Proactive defense against worst-case\n      reviewer concern about unidimensional IRT misfit.\n    priority: P1\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n  - id: plan_011\n    name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n    claim: 1\n    description: >\n      Inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix and measure\n      DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Addresses ground truth\n      circularity concern (Li et al. proxy labels measure existence, not differential contamination).\n      Design:\n      (a) Select 500 items with known-clean status (no DIF flags, stable across cohorts)\n      (b) For a random subset of 100-200 items, inject synthetic contamination:\n          flip responses of focal-group models from incorrect→correct with probability p\n          (simulating contamination at varying exploitation rates)\n      (c) Run DIF detection; measure TPR/FPR against known-injected ground truth\n      (d) Vary injection parameters: contamination prevalence (5%-30%), exploitation rate\n          (0.4-1.0), focal group size (N=40-200)\n      Provides controlled ground truth that proxy labels (Li et al.) and GSM1K cannot.\n    priority: P1\n    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n    deps: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: \"\"\n\n# GPU Budget Summary:\n# P0: 250 hours (plan_001: 0h + plan_002: 100h + plan_003a: 80h + plan_003b: 70h + plan_003c: 0h)\n# P1: 350 hours (plan_004: 100h + plan_005: 50h + plan_006: 200h + plan_010: 0h + plan_011: 0h)\n# P2: 0 hours (all simulation/analysis)\n# Total: 600 hours (within 900-1200h project budget, leaving margin for reruns)\n# Note: plan_010 (arch-stratified DIF) and plan_011 (semi-synthetic validation) added post-Dream,\n# both 0 GPU-h (CPU-only analysis on existing METABENCH data)\n# Claim 2 resilience: if one signal fails, 2-signal ensemble still publishable\n","numLines":264,"startLine":1,"totalLines":264}}}
